# Book Two — Wonder About Pi(e)
# Working arc for chambers 14 onward. Book One (01–13) carried an unspent wish to a
# furrow kept open "for whatever arrives next without a receipt". Book Two is what arrives.
# Plot is flexible; Boof's characterization is not; the Egg stays an open question.
#>book_two
#:story #!book-two #!working

^"book_two"{
  .title: "Wonder About Pi(e)"
  .status: "working"
  .chambers: "14 →"
  premise: "The Genie paid for Boof's wish with everything he had left, plus one Thursday. The Thursday goes missing from every person's week and stays in the field. On the day nobody kept, someone arrives through the warm furrow without a receipt — a figure in a few plain lines under a hat that never lands."
  shared_question: "What happens to a wish after it leaves the person who made it?"
  book_question: "Can a figure made to signify something become a person?"
  &boonhonk: ~".spw/story-boonhonk.spw#lore_land_story_boonhonk"
  &egg: ~".spw/surfaces/dragon-egg.spw#dragons_egg"
}

^"host_vector"{
  .note: "The plot the book's people are pursuing. WAPboy crosses it at a right angle (story-boonhonk#trope_physics)."
  feast: "Bonk City's Feast of the Uneven Slice at the Turning of the Year, where the one destined to spill exactly one pie is foretold to come to the table."
  hunt: "The city wants the destined one found before the feast — some to stop the spill, some to crown it."
  floating_title: "The chosen title floats like the hat. It lands on the wrong person for one scene at a time without becoming false."
  orthogonal: "The figure attends to whoever the feast overlooks: an ant whose torch went out, a porter holding a gate, the person who washes the plates."
}

^"threads_paid"{
  .note: "Book One plants that Book Two collects. Collect by placement, never by explanation."
  thursday: "chs 03, 05, 08, 12, 13 — a small wish costs a coincidence and a Thursday; ch14 is the Thursday."
  coincidence: "ch07 — every spent wish owes a coincidence to a stranger; ch12 — a stranger would receive a good coincidence. Left unassigned on purpose."
  furrow: "ch12 — one warm furrow kept open, always, for whatever arrives next without a receipt; ch14 — something does."
  hat: "ch13 — a hat at a person's height, touching nothing, farther down the road each time she looked."
  slice: "ch06 → ch10 — the warm BoonWAP slice Boof has never eaten; still warm in ch14. Carried, never explained."
  button: "ch06 — somebody in the plaza lost a button; ch15 — the figure retrieves it from under the parcel gate."
  bell: "ch01 far cart bell → ch13 nearby → ch14 arrives on the crier's cart."
}

^"chambers"{
  c14: {
    title: "The Thursday Nobody Kept"
    signal: "first_signal + field_signal + orthogonal_signal (story-boonhonk#teaser_grammar)"
    beats: "no person is in the day → the berries correct a quarter turn → ruled ant-streets → the hat above the furrow, a drawing under it facing ninety degrees away → a dead torch relit from the end of a line arm; the arm stays shorter → straight across country, hedge left with a rectangular gap → Friday arrives with everyone in it → the crier announces the feast; mistakes him for a scarecrow; writes that she saw a hat"
    remainder: "a straight ant-street with a torch that was a little of somebody's arm"
  }
  c15: {
    title: "Pupil, Parcel, or Door"
    signal: "second_signal — records disagree; all three remain valid"
    beats: "a straight line comes out at the parcel gate where no road arrives → the porter must shut the gate before the storm; the figure fits under the lintel and the hat does not → the porter holds the gate; the feast flour is spoiled; he signs the damage in his own name → the button → gate-book (parcel), repair log (door), night-school roll (pupil: the night school is for grown-ups after the day's work) → Mx. BoneWAP posts all three → by one o'clock the city decides the destined one is the dog"
    remainder: "three receipts pinned side by side; a lintel marked where a hat very nearly touched it"
    care: "children may be in the story, never configured so a parent's protective instinct flinches: the stranger is never placed among children or enrolled as one; an adult does not handle someone else's child; a child is not promoted into a title, card or collectible. The intergenerational note is kept where it belongs: a father sharing his folio with his own children (ch6), and a night school grown-ups attend whether or not they have children (ch15)"
  }
  c16: {
    .status: "next stone — 2026-11-13"
    title_seed: "The Second Place"
    seed: "Boof wears the floating title for one scene at BoonWAP's table. The figure, at the back, dries plates beside the washer-up nobody has asked the name of (story-boonhonk#boonhonk_anchor_last_slice). If the pie is never smaller, why does the person serving it still get tired?"
  }
}

^"sightings"{
  .note: "Play loop (book/scripts/modules/sightings.mjs): nine sightings, nine strokes of the plain drawing. Noticing one — a margin rumour surfacing, or a prose passage read — earns a stroke; the drawing never earns a name."
  margins: "sighting-sand 03 head · sighting-records 04 body · sighting-hat 06 hat · sighting-corners 09 left leg · sighting-orthogonal 11 right leg · sighting-scent 12 left eye"
  prose: "road 13 left arm · furrow 14 right arm (drawn shorter: the torch) · button 15 right eye"
  where_seen: "a settle card on the chapter (switch: sightings, default on); the home hat drawer; the desk's ?[sightings]"
  extend: "a later sighting adds no strokes — nine is whole. New sightings can redraw (a moved line, a missing dot) once the story earns a choice."
}

^"exactly_one_pie"{
  .note: "A small game at /pie/ (book/scripts/home/pie-game.mjs, book/styles/home/pie.css; PLAN.E L28). The prophecy as a thing you can hold."
  premise: "Someone is destined to spill exactly one pie. A figure in a hat carries one, level, through the book, chapter by chapter, while the land streams down at him."
  voyage: "the cabinet holds still and the book goes by it, like a ship through the book's own space: each chapter is a leg of the carry in the place it happens (the frosted field for the Boon.land chapters, the lanes, the plaza for Six, the rented court for Four and Eleven, the long table for Thirteen, the parcel gate for Fifteen), as long as its prose; its words (variant zero) flow past on one side, the passage now passing level with the carrier; its wonders wait on the other. After Fifteen, the book again, faster. Started from a chapter page (or ?chapter=), or chosen from the chapter leg selector on the start card, the carry starts there"
  ahead: "four steps up the lanes as well as five across (↑ ↓ W S, swipes any way): up is where the wonders wait, and where what is coming comes sooner"
  orthogonal: "He never goes the way the scene goes; he steps across it, one lane-length at a time — a line segment with a right-angle corner tick (∟), the ghost of where he was (story-boonhonk.spw#trope_physics orthogonal_motion, quest)"
  drawing: "always the same plain ink drawing, in every binding: one circle, one rectangle, four lines, two dots (and from Chapter Fourteen onward, the right arm a little shorter and warm at the tip); the hat lags behind him and never falls, not even at the spill"
  wobble: "the tilt is the one thing at stake: every step tilts the pie, steps in a hurry most; it settles if left alone — feeling as tilt and spacing, not as a face. Stepping into the direction of a lean catches the plate under the tip (CAUGHT · STEADIED); holding still in a lane without tilting earns poise (POISED), which steadies the pie faster and makes the next throw a long, level poised throw. A ring beside the pie and the level under the screen (with a directional lean bead) show it; full, it spills"
  knocks: "a hazard wears a red ring at its foot and knocks rather than ends the carry: it tilts the pie by what hit it, said as it happens (TILT +50%), the porter taking it best and a clown worst; some take more — crown-wasps sting the filling, gate-pigeons peck the crust, a spool trips you a lane, the dividers measure off a little of both. A breath of grace after, the carrier blinking, the screen shaken and reddened; the spill is blamed on the last knock. Slips wear a blue dashed ring: puddles tilt, frost slides the next step a lane further, a bag of flour sets the story back. A slice shared with a feast clerk settles the damage slip (SETTLED · NO DAMAGE); a slice shared with a court subject lets them step aside (+ CRUST)"
  boof: "crosses the lanes on her own business now and then; brushing past her steadies the pie. She is the materially specific animal beside the drawn figure; she works for the land, and helps in passing"
  places: "six distinct architectural grounds that cross-fade at chapter seams: the frosted field (furrow-ruled turf, field stones, frost, warm furrows); the parcel gate in a storm (wet slate flags and threshold iron); the lanes, which curve for everyone (running-bond cobbles and Bonk glass on its edge); the plaza, where the pie holds court (radial rings and brass studs); the rented court (checkerboard marble tiles and ledger lines); the long table, where a second place is set (linen runner, wood grain, and place-setting brass) — the empty place is the only way through. Said under the screen as the carry crosses into each chapter"
  the_end: "always exactly one spill; the card says EXACTLY ONE., what the land would say about the cause, and how far he carried it. The prophecy holds; the distance is the reader's"
  naming: "he is never named on the page; the game calls him a figure in a hat"
  under_the_hat: "it is not always him. The chosen title floats like the hat and may be worn by the wrong person for a scene (story-boonhonk.spw chosen_one): each carry the hat settles on someone — the figure in plain lines, the porter from the parcel gate (steady, slow), a feast clerk (quick, nervous), the Genie off-season (glides), a clown (not to be trusted with a pie) — and at a cut it may move on. The others are drawn in their materials; only he is plain ink"
  wap: "clowns love WAP — Bonk City's word for a pie in the face, after the sound it makes. Clowns drift toward the pie; step into one and WAP: it gets the pie square, loves it, and the pie on the plate is no smaller (one pie that remains whole after being served). A WAP jostles the plate; it is not a spill. A clown carrying the pie that tips too far gives itself WAP, which counts as the spill"
  sound: "all sounds are synthesized via Web Audio (no external files), only after the reader starts a carry and when the toggle is on: WAP splat and horn honks, soft cobble footsteps, counter-step catches, hops and deft landings, Bonk glass chimes, bronze post hums, berry chimes, wonder arpeggios, and the spill"
  quinary_five: "Mr. BoonWAP, a sentient pie of the quinary five (spwashi.com, RPG Wednesday: the quinary story bible), holds court at the head of the lanes: clowns flock, every pie finds an eye (a wider reach), and he lobs slices of himself into theirs; he leaves still talking after the room has moved on. Mr. BaneWAP, his foil, meddles with an announced scheme — a rented wind that shoves the carry a lane, a tax that doubles every step's tilt, the lanes re-let under new management — and, a proper cartoon villain, leaves a visible off switch on the way through; step on it, or throw at it in the gold moment, and he is foiled. They never share the stage; the plaza leans boon, the rented court bane"
  throw: "a slice (Space X, the throw button, or a click or tap right where); the pie on the plate is never smaller, the next slice takes a second. WAPs close together are a combo: the horn climbs, the pie steadies, and from three a slice is ready at once"
  thin: "short of flour or boonberries, a slice goes without them — crust and no filling, filling and no crust, or crumbs — and the clown is satisfied anyway; a thin WAP counts once, never twice"
  flour: "flour never spills the pie and never slows the carrier: a bag feeds the crust and pushes the carry back in the story — 70 cobbles back along this chapter, or, early in a chapter, back into the last; a fifteen-dot track at the top shows where the carry is"
  rows: "the land comes in rows; each row leaves a way through no more than one lane from the last; drifting things stay within a fifth of a lane of home; hazards hit a little smaller than they look, friends a little larger; the pace builds from 150 by the minute and the chapter, and rows keep a set distance so the time to react holds"
  wonders: "each chapter's own wonders (almanac.json, up to six: its own first, then its echoes, the unkept before the kept) wait a moment up the lanes, then go about their business. Steered into, one is kept in the almanac and gives the carry something: torch-ants and salt-whales light the way through, echo-wrens echo the next throws, the lag-stag lags the land and not the carrier, jazz-geese honk the way clear, the hob-deer and the page-fox leave the carrier unkept so nothing can hold them for a moment, pane-snails level the pie; the rest give by kind"
  hop: "a hop (Space, the hop button, a tap on the carrier) clears low things — stones, holes, snails, spools, puddles, frost, flour, a fallen banner — for good, and readies a slice; posts, glass, tables and people are too tall. Clearing a hazard right at the hop's peak is a deft hop (DEFT HOP · STEADIED), settling the pie on landing. Holes are freshly dug in the field and a cobble gone in the lanes: in and out again costs a step back down the lanes. A ditch across every lane has no way round, only over. A slice thrown at the top of a hop is lofted as if off a ramp"
  appetite: "each clown wants so much pie, shown as pips over its head (seconds more often further into the book). A whole slice fills one pip; a rich one a pip and a half, a full pie two; a Perfect twice that; a slice short of crust or filling half, crumbs a quarter. Satisfied, a clown goes home delighted and tosses back whatever the aura is shortest of; wanting, it keeps its pie-face and follows the pie; past the pie still hungry, it goes off wanting more"
  rich: "the pies grow with the aura: with plenty of flour and boonberries the pie in hand swells on the plate (with scalloped crust flutes, berry vents, and rising steam) and each slice is bigger, reaches wider, and satisfies more"
  slow: "slowing is a gift, never a penalty: a slow beaver ambles through the lanes now and then, and steered into, the land goes at its pace for a while and the carrier does not (the lag-stag does the same)"
  flour_in_the_air: "a bag burst, a slice into a bag, a pile kicked up, a march: flour in the air, a pale haze. It feeds the two pies — Mr. BoonWAP throws faster, pours crust and lingers; Mr. BaneWAP's scheme quickens and lasts — and draws them sooner"
  mimes: "a mime works a lane, palms flat on a wall in the next: invisible, and also there (a shimmer and two gloves show it). Mimes Against WAP march across the lanes in silence, one gap, placards blank but one (MAW; a pie, crossed out). Both box any flat slice thrown into them, while a lofted slice sails clean over (OVER THE MIMES). The march shakes flour off its placards into the air for Mr. BaneWAP, whose newest scheme is to organise one ('they agreed with me, silently'); flour from mimes usually brings him, and if Mr. BoonWAP is holding court, he eats it instead. Foiled, BaneWAP takes the mimes home with him"
  baneberries: "Mr. BaneWAP is made of baneberries, as Mr. BoonWAP is filled with boonberries: plum-black, a cold violet sheen, a thorn on the stem. They lie thicker while he is about and where mimes are; a mime gathers any nearby for him (in pantomime, and they go all the same), and while he is here any that get past the pie are his. Each one makes his scheme last and quicken, or, while he is away, brings him sooner and fuller. Stepped on, one is kept from him. Hit with a slice first, a baneberry's core is sweetened (SWEETENED) — stepped on after, it feeds the pie's filling instead of BaneWAP (Something under his crust went sweet at the bridge, ch12: baneberry, with a sweetened core)"
  learning: "the book introduces the lanes as it goes: each thing first comes up the lanes in the chapter the story is ready for it (field things from One; ditches, puddles, slow beavers from Two; the lanes' bronze, glass and flour from Three; the court, crown-wasps and baneberries from Four; mimes from Five; clerks, dividers and Mimes Against WAP from Six), anywhere once met; Mr. BaneWAP arrives from Four and Mr. BoonWAP from Six, as in the book. A first meeting is said under the screen with what the thing does, and a NEW rides over it while it is in view"
  rhythm: "each chapter builds toward its end; a section break in the words is a breath in the lanes — a § rule across them and a row or two with nothing to knock"
  carries: "every chapter carried through keeps a mark in the reader's browser (pie-carries.mjs): how the carry went there as boon (clowns satisfied, wonders kept, warm things met), bane (knocks, slices boxed, baneberries brought to Mr. BaneWAP) and bone (hops, perfect throws, slices lofted over the mimes). On the chapter page the reading leans a little that way through the lens weights (pie-projection.mjs), never over a lens the reader set or a link gave; a note at the top says how and why, with a switch to read plainly and a door back into the carry. Beside the screen, 'so far' shows the tally as it goes; the game page lists every chapter's mark, with the switch and a way to forget"
  mimes_win: "a contest, once Mimes Against WAP are in a chapter: the pie (clowns satisfied, slices over the mimes, baneberries sweetened or squashed, Mr. BaneWAP foiled) against the mimes (slices boxed, baneberries gathered for him, knocks, a march that gets past, his march run its course). While the mimes lead it is harder to wonder about pie: the passage's pie words go hollow, wonders show only as a shimmer until close, and Mr. BoonWAP holds court in mime. Finish the chapter with them ahead and they hold it: its pie words go quiet on its page too, until a carry through it out-does them (a held chapter starts with them two ahead)"
  mimes_reach: "a held chapter makes it harder to wonder about pie beyond the game too: on the entrance the pie drawer wears a mimed wall and resists the first knock, the land's riffle passes it by, and it names the held chapter with a door to win it back (wonder.mjs); the carry band says which chapters they hold (app.mjs); the chapter's pie doors are drawn dashed, 'held by the mimes' (pie-segues.mjs); ?[pie] at the desk lists every chapter's lean and hold (scriptorium-desk.mjs)"
  reciprocity: "the carry and the book's collectibles feed each other, all kept in this browser and all listed beside the screen ('kept in this browser', 'in play from your shelves') and under the carries: sightings — each noticed stroke steadies the figure in plain lines (settle +2.5% a stroke), and carrying the pie past a prose sighting (Thirteen, Fourteen, Fifteen) notices it; the almanac — a wonder already kept waits longer up the lanes and gives a fifth more; the bench — the latest kept combine rides as a charm by its bracket: [window] lights the way longer, {room} poises sooner and settles faster, (gathering) reaches wider for friends, <passage> steadies and feeds the aura at each new chapter; other tellings — a living slot in the passage turns toward the carry's lean as it passes (or at a tap) and is kept as heard (lore.tellings.v1)"
  learning_catch: "the first time the pie leans hard to one side, a caption teaches the catch: step once toward the lean to steady the plate"
  egg_shaped: "now and then, at the very top of the lanes, something warm and egg-shaped, likeliest in One, Eleven, Thirteen and Fourteen. It is not for taking and it is not explained (the game never calls it the Egg). Passed close, the pie settles faster for a while and the next knock is forgiven"
  doors: "gate (Chapter Fifteen, the parcel gate) · lanes (Chapter Six, the Fool's Labyrinth) · plaza (Chapter Six, the pie at the table) · court (Chapter Eleven, the wish that would not finish) · table (Chapter Fifteen, a second place set): each door starts the voyage in its chapter"
  lore: "what the pies let slip (book/content/world/pie-lore.json): Mr. BoonWAP says one true thing about the land each time he holds court; Mr. BaneWAP, foiled, blurts one. Lines unlock with what the visitor has found across the site (chapters read, a chapter read, wonders kept, sightings, tellings heard) and done in the lanes (WAPs, perfects, foils, shared slices, sweetened baneberries, the furthest chapter reached). Each grounds itself in a chapter. The page keeps the said, the ready, and the locked with how to unlock them. Rules: the Egg stays a mystery; the figure under the hat is never named; the warm slice is never explained; 'there are five of us; everyone counts four' is as near as anyone comes"
  caption: "places and the pies' words are said under the screen, like subtitles; a line stays, quieter, until the next — never across the carry"
  aim: "throws do not home: a needle sweeps from the pie and a slice flies where it points (or toward a hovered pointer; a click or tap sends it right there), with a multi-segment trajectory line previewing bounces, curves, and ramps. A target's ring turns gold when the trajectory lines up with its face. Perfect: dead centre, off a wall, off Bonk glass, around a post, or off a ramp. Bronze posts curve a slice toward the nearest waiting face (AROUND THE POST); Bonk glass banks a slice at the speed of light and remembers the impact by loosing a shard-slice at the nearest target (THE GLASS REMEMBERS); walls bounce it (each bounce costs reach); crown-wasps and gate-pigeons snatch it; anything solid takes it with a splat; a rented wind drifts it"
  aura: "the pie in hand stays whole because an aura keeps replenishing it, from two ingredients: flour (the crust) and boonberries (the filling, teal and amber lit from within, in the lanes). Every slice draws on both; a wasted slice costs more; run either dry and the slices go thin, and a thin pie tips more easily. Mr. BoonWAP's benevolence: filling poured back in, a steadying word, wasted slices caught"
  flour_and_ramps: "flour comes in bags (crust, a setback in the story) and piles (a little crust). A pile is a ramp: a slice over it is lofted clean over the next thing; piles in a row stack the loft. A slice thrown into a bag bursts it into a pile — composing a ramp — and is not wasted"
  legend: "the page explains every thing with sprites drawn by the game's own hand, each with what it costs: knocks the pie (with its tilt), slips and setbacks, go toward, two of the quinary five, under the hat"
  segues: "pie-segues.mjs: where a chapter reaches a door's place, a door at the section's end carries the pie from there (?scene= &from=); the ending points to the passage that was going by when it spilled, and the way back returns to the very section the game was found in, where a note says how the carry went"
}

^"pie_voices"{
  .note: "How Mr. BoonWAP and Mr. BaneWAP speak, in the chapters and in Exactly One Pie, so that each line they say can carry a piece of the land's lore (canon of the quinary five: spwashi.com play/rpg-wednesday, the quinary story bible)."
  boonwap: "talks past the room: answers questions nobody asked, takes a nod for agreement, beams a long way; and in every speech one true thing about the land. He misses what the room is telling him — the server's wrist, the washing-up — and the prose lets the reader see it (ch6). He is generous without noticing what it costs or what it fixed (ch12)."
  banewap: "announces: a scheme is not a scheme until it is declared to someone who did not ask. Every scheme carries its off switch — a clause he wrote himself and never reads (ch11). Foiled, he always says one thing more than he means to (“The crown was wasps.”). Re-sweetened at the bridge, he schemes from habit, and asks, in the form of a threat, to be treated kindly (ch12)."
  in_the_chapters: "ch6 the pie at the table (never smaller: only arithmetic); ch11 the announcement and the clause, the blurt; ch12 the bridge (I have always said so; bitter starting tomorrow unless); ch15 the long table (the second place is for whoever it was set for)"
  in_the_game: "the game's lore lines (book/content/world/pie-lore.json) are what they say off the page in the same shapes: BoonWAP's while he holds court, BaneWAP's when foiled; each line grounds itself in the chapter where its shape, or its fact, can be read"
  rule: "neither of them decodes anything: the Egg stays a mystery, the figure under the hat is unnamed, the warm slice unexplained; the nearest either comes is BoonWAP's 'there are five of us; everyone counts four'"
}

?"open"[
  "Who receives the good coincidence the small wish owed a stranger?"
  "What does the hat remember?"
  "Whose arm-length of line is burning in the ant-street, and does it grow back?"
  "Is the empty shell at the archive still warm because of what left it, or what is coming?"
  "Who is destined to spill exactly one pie — and is destiny something you can be mistaken for?"
]

~"guards"{
  wapboy: "Never decode the drawing in prose. The name WAPboy does not appear in chapter text until a choice earns it."
  egg: "Do not say what hatched, or whether anything did. The shell stays warm and empty."
  fool: "Sidelined. Book Two does not lean on the Fool."
  boof: "Works for the land, not for pay or fruit; appreciates blueberries without liking them; nose before name."
  compass: "No cardinal directions — dawnward, hearthward, duskward, by day or destination."
  ledger: "Records appear as comedy and as care, never as the engine."
}

^"plates"{
  .rule: "A Book Two plate is a painted scene with the figure drawn into it in plain lines — the mismatch is the canon (story-boonhonk#wapboy scene_relation)."
  .status: "study — drawn over 2026-03-01 seed paintings until a painted plate is grounded"
  p14: "lone tree under a teal storm; a stream bends, the hat goes straight up the slope"
  p15: "Bonk City's snowed back lanes at night; the figure with a parcel tag, a lit seam like a door, a button in one hand"
}
